🌿 3. Environmental Assessment Uncertainties & Model Limitations
While the sustainability model relies on recognized international standards (EEA, DEFRA, Thermo King), several real-world operational variables introduce inherent calculation limits:
A. Telemetry Data Availability & Sensor Penetration
Phase 1 Limitation: Direct real-time CAN-bus fuel consumption feeds are not globally available across all third-party fleet units.
Model Treatment: Fuel savings are computed using Thermo King SB-210 baseline technical specifications (2.50 L/hr active cooling burn rate) rather than live tank telemetry. Direct IoT fuel sensor integration is deferred to Phase 2 MRV auditing.
B. Variability of Physics & Emission Factors
Phase 1 Limitation: The stoichiometric emission factor (2.68 kg CO2/Liter) assumes standard commercial EN 590 diesel combustion.
Model Treatment: Variations in localized biodiesel blends (B7 vs. B10), engine wear, and extreme ambient temperature fluctuations (which alter reefer cooling compressor duty cycles) can cause a ±8% variance in actual tailpipe emissions.
C. Dynamic Corridor Operating Conditions
Phase 1 Limitation: Staging queue delays ($T_{\text{baseline}} = 42.50\text{ hours}$) are highly dynamic and subject to external macro disruptions.
Model Treatment: Future queue lengths, seasonal harvest surges, border blockades, or customs staffing changes will alter the absolute volume of avoided idling hours. The model treats $T_{\text{baseline}}$ as a dynamic telemetry variable updated via daily ZMPD/Border Guard reports rather than a static constant.
